    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_90ac7f6635a9e17a3c9094c0c3289cc6272f84ad121e29c2ac579dd9fda6757c",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The semi-local quotient supplies a common Hilbert space for additive Fourier transform and multiplicative scaling, and the dual cutoff trace converts the Fourier multiplier's logarithmic derivative into local principal-value terms. Poisson summation with self-dual lattice normalization explains both the local-factor ratio and the disappearance, on the quotient, of the remaining basic-character ambiguity; applying a Fourier-compatible scaling differential to the Poisson distribution then produces the simultaneous support gaps used in Sonine-space constructions.",
